Enums with state and behaviour.

In many languages, an enum is just a list of named integers. But Java's are full objects. Each constant can carry its own fields, and the enum itself can have constructors and methods. That single fact turns the enum from a labelled number into a compact, type-safe way to attach data and behaviour to a fixed set of things.

Let's start with constants that carry data. You give the enum some fields and a constructor, and then pass each constant its own values in parentheses. Take a planet enum. Mercury is declared with its mass and radius; earth with its own mass and radius. The enum has two final fields, mass and radius, and a constructor that assigns them. One important detail: the constructor is always private — you never write new on an enum constant, because the JVM creates them for you. Now the enum has a gravity method, shared by all constants, that computes gravity from mass and radius. So earth dot gravity gives you earth's gravity. Earth isn't a bare label any more — it knows its own mass and radius, and can compute its own gravity, with that data living right on the constant, immutable, defined once.

Sometimes, though, each constant should behave differently. For that, you declare an abstract method on the enum, and let each constant supply its own body. Picture an operation enum. Plus provides an apply method that adds; times provides an apply method that multiplies; and the enum declares apply as abstract. So operation dot times dot apply, of three and four, gives you twelve, because it dispatches to the times body. Each constant is effectively its own tiny subclass. And here's the payoff: add a minus constant, and the compiler forces you to give it an apply too. The behaviour can't be forgotten, because it lives on the constant itself. That's much cleaner than a switch statement you'd have to remember to update every time.

One more capability. An enum can't extend a class, because it already extends Enum behind the scenes. But it can implement interfaces. So an operation enum could implement a binary-operator interface, letting each constant slot straight into code that expects that capability. Enums participate in polymorphism, just like any other type.

So when should you reach for a rich enum? It's perfect for a fixed set of strategies or categories that each carry data or logic — units of measurement with conversion factors, HTTP status categories, discount rules, or the states of a state machine with their allowed transitions. The moment your list of constants also needs "and each one has a rate, or a label, or a rule," put that data and behaviour on the enum itself, rather than in scattered switch statements or parallel maps. It stays in one place, immutable, and exhaustively checked. Records, coming up next, do the same trick, but for open-ended immutable data — data whose values are not a fixed set.
